    "Documented dembow convention — the 3+3+2 riddim figure as taught in published production guides; no recording was transcribed"
  ],
  "notes": "One figure runs the whole record: the dembow. Kick on 1 and 3, a backbeat under it, and a rim/snare marking the a-of-1 and the and-of-2 twice a bar — the boom-ch-boom-chick that repeats bar after bar with almost no variation. Because the snare barely moves, everything that keeps a loop alive is somewhere else: the hats switch resolution, the congas, bongos, timbales, shaker and cowbell are redrawn every bar, and the open hats wander. Harmony is a four-chord minor loop with moderate sevenths, the topline is syllabic and sits mid register, and the 808 mirrors the kick rather than writing a line of its own. Confidence is medium: the compendium reaches only as far as the dancehall-influenced mainstream (ch. 2 §10), so the dembow placement here comes from documented convention rather than in-repo research, and the tempo and percussion numbers are extrapolated from that section's 90–120 BPM band and its clave-locked interlocking-part culture.",
